CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: mechanism
SCALE_REALISM: Does not yet hold at realistic workload. Evidence is a deterministic L0 trace simulation with synthetic salience and success=referenced-span availability, not real attention/citation prediction or task accuracy. ACON already demonstrates real agent benchmarks and token reductions, so this packet must show real tool-output sparsity/predictability and matched real-model task accuracy before promotion.
RATIONALE: PROMPT_VERSION v001. The packet gives strong simulated support for the narrow mechanism: EC beats RT in 633/648 cells with CI [+0.275,+0.307], and EO-EC gap is 0 in sparse+high-predictability. That is a useful mechanism result, not mere glue. But the systems claim is not green because the premise is constructed: ref sparsity, predictability, and salience are synthetic, and task success is availability rather than model accuracy. Prior art is crowded and includes ACON, which directly targets long-horizon agent context compression on AppWorld/OfficeBench/Multi-objective QA and reports 26-54% peak-token reduction plus task-success improvements (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00615). H2O/SnapKV/StreamingLLM/LLMLingua cover attention/token/KV selection mechanisms with real models, while vLLM/LMCache/TensorRT-LLM cover shipping cache infrastructure. No located shipping system exactly implements "tool-result-specific extractive retention via causal future-reference prediction with oracle-gap accounting", but closest OSS already covers most surrounding systems surface. Required evidence: real attention/citation predictor AUC for future tool-output references, real tool-agent benchmark accuracy at matched budgets, comparison to ACON/LLMLingua/H2O/SnapKV/StreamingLLM-style baselines, and reproducible GPU traces showing latency/memory/token effects.
